  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "This envelope can carry two distinct signature-bearing advertisements for the same chunk and keeps evidence peer-local, but it leaves chunk semantics, advertiser identity, and Bob's peer-choice accounting to higher layers, so it only partially answers the scenario.",
    "strengths": [
      "Mandatory signature bytes over canonical [pcid,payload] let Bob preserve separate local records of Alice's and Carol's offers.",
      "The design does not force a central reputation service or registry; peer choice can remain a local policy above the envelope.",
      "Explicit pCID dispatch and envelope/payload separation keep duplicate-advertisement semantics in the payload protocol instead of hidden wire conventions."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "No chunk-advertisement payload, freshness model, or promise-accounting record is defined, so Bob's actual peer-selection logic is out of scope.",
      "Signature bytes are opaque and signer/verification semantics are external, which limits standalone human/LLM auditability.",
      "The encoding prose mentions sig_pcid/sig_payload when present despite the declared 3-slot envelope, leaving auth evolution details unclear."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Best-effort inspection of unknown pCIDs could tempt operators to act on unsupported duplicate offers as if they were verified.",
      "Out-of-band signature interpretation could drift into incompatible or centralized trust conventions across ecosystems."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "What payload schema identifies chunk C, advertiser identity, and offer freshness so duplicate ads can be compared locally?",
      "How should Bob record peer-local promise accounting and transfer outcomes without turning the feed into shared reputation?",
      "How is signature algorithm or version discovery handled over long-term evolution if the envelope only carries opaque signature bytes?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
}
